WEBVTT

0
00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:01.039
As a manager,

1
00:00:01.179 --> 00:00:03.549
one of your key roles related to compensation

2
00:00:03.650 --> 00:00:06.449
is to support and facilitate pay conversations

3
00:00:06.449 --> 00:00:08.430
with your direct reports as appropriate.

4
00:00:08.609 --> 00:00:09.369
In this section,

5
00:00:09.529 --> 00:00:12.670
we will walk through a few tips and tricks to support you in this role.

6
00:00:13.529 --> 00:00:14.159
Once again,

7
00:00:14.279 --> 00:00:18.469
your HR partners are available to support you as you prepare for pay discussions.

8
00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:21.040
Please connect with them for additional resources or

9
00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:23.260
guidance specific to your school or unit.

10
00:00:23.559 --> 00:00:26.670
Before we get too far into our discussion on pay conversations,

11
00:00:26.989 --> 00:00:28.879
let's take a look back to the information

12
00:00:28.879 --> 00:00:32.080
we discussed earlier about pay transparency at Stanford.

13
00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:35.799
This will be helpful to keep in mind if you are having compensation discussions.

14
00:00:36.095 --> 00:00:37.384
With your direct reports.

15
00:00:37.534 --> 00:00:38.525
As a reminder,

16
00:00:38.974 --> 00:00:42.735
pay transparency at Stanford is focused on effectively communicating pay

17
00:00:42.735 --> 00:00:44.924
decisions to staff in a clear and informed way.

18
00:00:45.095 --> 00:00:48.884
We enable pay transparency by making Stanford's compensation policies,

19
00:00:49.055 --> 00:00:49.834
procedures

20
00:00:50.095 --> 00:00:53.044
and assistance services available and accessible.

21
00:00:53.235 --> 00:00:55.974
Stanford also supports pay transparency by including a

22
00:00:55.974 --> 00:00:58.255
job market pay range on every job posting.

23
00:00:58.669 --> 00:01:01.470
And employees are entitled to request and receive the

24
00:01:01.470 --> 00:01:03.500
job market pay range for their current position.

25
00:01:03.750 --> 00:01:07.470
Please remember that individual compensation information is sensitive

26
00:01:07.470 --> 00:01:09.690
information that needs to be managed appropriately.

27
00:01:09.870 --> 00:01:13.150
While you can discuss an employee's pay and pay range with them,

28
00:01:13.550 --> 00:01:15.169
you should never share or discuss

29
00:01:15.169 --> 00:01:17.930
the compensation information of another employee.

30
00:01:18.230 --> 00:01:19.410
Pay transparency.

31
00:01:19.809 --> 00:01:23.379
Especially the national shift toward including the expected reasonable

32
00:01:23.379 --> 00:01:25.730
pay range for the job in a job posting

33
00:01:25.900 --> 00:01:28.819
has enabled job candidates and current employees to be

34
00:01:28.819 --> 00:01:32.440
more informed and have deeper conversations about their compensation.

35
00:01:32.620 --> 00:01:35.599
This means managers must be prepared to address questions

36
00:01:35.739 --> 00:01:38.900
and help employees understand the information within the context

37
00:01:38.900 --> 00:01:42.449
of the key factors that influence individual pay decisions.

38
00:01:42.580 --> 00:01:44.400
In the spirit of transparency,

39
00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:46.980
employees will likely seek to understand.

40
00:01:47.620 --> 00:01:48.660
Why their pay

41
00:01:48.940 --> 00:01:49.940
is what it is,

42
00:01:50.209 --> 00:01:51.330
relative to the market,

43
00:01:51.419 --> 00:01:54.180
whether their pay is equitable compared to their peers,

44
00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:56.129
how performance factors into pay,

45
00:01:56.220 --> 00:01:58.010
how their pay level is determined.

46
00:01:58.139 --> 00:02:00.069
We've discussed many important aspects of

47
00:02:00.069 --> 00:02:02.760
Stanford's compensation philosophy to help you

48
00:02:03.169 --> 00:02:04.089
understand the basics,

49
00:02:04.099 --> 00:02:06.160
to be able to address these questions.

50
00:02:06.540 --> 00:02:11.059
Your local HR partners are also here to support you when questions like these arise.

51
00:02:11.339 --> 00:02:13.050
Please Consult with them in preparation

52
00:02:13.050 --> 00:02:15.399
for compensation conversations with employees.

53
00:02:15.570 --> 00:02:17.570
It's also important to remember that

54
00:02:17.850 --> 00:02:20.199
when discussing compensation with employees,

55
00:02:20.529 --> 00:02:23.669
to be cautious when an employee shares compensation data

56
00:02:23.809 --> 00:02:25.289
that they have sourced themselves.

57
00:02:25.490 --> 00:02:25.949
Often,

58
00:02:26.289 --> 00:02:28.949
this data comes from self-reported platforms

59
00:02:29.130 --> 00:02:30.770
rather than comprehensive,

60
00:02:31.009 --> 00:02:32.270
reliable sources.

61
00:02:32.550 --> 00:02:36.029
Stanford's data is rigorously researched and annually updated

62
00:02:36.250 --> 00:02:37.529
and therefore should be used in

63
00:02:37.529 --> 00:02:40.070
place of employee source compensation information.

64
00:02:40.470 --> 00:02:43.660
Your HR partners can help ensure you have the correct information.

65
00:02:43.960 --> 00:02:44.429
Now,

66
00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:46.600
let's walk through some guidance to help you have

67
00:02:46.600 --> 00:02:50.039
transparent and informed pay conversations with your direct reports.

68
00:02:50.279 --> 00:02:52.380
Discussing pay is not always easy

69
00:02:52.520 --> 00:02:54.279
and sometimes the conversation can feel

70
00:02:54.279 --> 00:02:56.750
uncomfortable for both managers and employees.

71
00:02:56.919 --> 00:03:00.100
That's why compensation conversations should be handled with care

72
00:03:00.410 --> 00:03:01.240
and you

73
00:03:01.399 --> 00:03:02.380
as a manager,

74
00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:05.399
should take steps to prepare for the discussion in advance.

75
00:03:05.669 --> 00:03:08.270
The first step should be to identify the type

76
00:03:08.270 --> 00:03:11.330
of compensation conversation that needs to take place.

77
00:03:12.029 --> 00:03:12.589
Generally,

78
00:03:12.869 --> 00:03:16.270
there are 4 types of pay conversations that may arise.

79
00:03:17.100 --> 00:03:17.970
These include

80
00:03:18.699 --> 00:03:21.509
conversations to set compensation expectations with job

81
00:03:21.509 --> 00:03:23.740
candidates as part of the hiring process,

82
00:03:23.949 --> 00:03:26.669
initial compensation conversations when you are ready

83
00:03:26.669 --> 00:03:28.250
to make an offer to a candidate,

84
00:03:28.509 --> 00:03:33.179
annual merit or base salary increase discussions with existing employees,

85
00:03:33.190 --> 00:03:33.850
and

86
00:03:34.229 --> 00:03:36.839
pay conversations initiated by the employee.

87
00:03:37.259 --> 00:03:39.539
This is often a request for a pay raise

88
00:03:39.539 --> 00:03:42.220
or a conversation to better understand their current pay.

89
00:03:42.500 --> 00:03:45.220
We'll briefly look at each of these discussion types

90
00:03:45.220 --> 00:03:47.440
and offer a few key tips and tricks.

91
00:03:47.619 --> 00:03:49.339
It's also important to keep in mind that you may

92
00:03:49.339 --> 00:03:52.979
need to engage in compensation discussions related to job reclassification.

93
00:03:53.335 --> 00:03:54.324
or promotions,

94
00:03:54.574 --> 00:03:56.315
though these take place less frequently.

95
00:03:56.455 --> 00:03:59.455
If you have a team member who is experiencing either event

96
00:03:59.615 --> 00:04:02.044
and a compensation conversation is merited,

97
00:04:02.294 --> 00:04:04.574
consult with your HR partner for guidance.

98
00:04:04.735 --> 00:04:07.024
Let's start with compensation conversations.

99
00:04:07.175 --> 00:04:08.835
When you are hiring a new employee.

100
00:04:09.589 --> 00:04:13.050
The first critical step is to set compensation expectations

101
00:04:13.190 --> 00:04:15.029
with candidates early in the process.

102
00:04:15.190 --> 00:04:15.860
Remember,

103
00:04:16.108 --> 00:04:20.369
candidates will see the full job market salary range as part of the job posting

104
00:04:20.548 --> 00:04:24.290
and the full range is generally broader than the position's budgeted range.

105
00:04:24.510 --> 00:04:27.829
It's also important to remember that the job market salary range is based

106
00:04:27.829 --> 00:04:31.130
on the assumption that the employee will be working from a Stanford workload.

107
00:04:31.390 --> 00:04:32.660
Location in the Bay Area.

108
00:04:32.890 --> 00:04:35.200
If the position is open to remote employees,

109
00:04:35.399 --> 00:04:38.040
it will be important that remote candidates understand that the

110
00:04:38.040 --> 00:04:41.619
range will be adjusted to reflect Stanford's location-based pay approach.

111
00:04:41.799 --> 00:04:44.149
To prepare for expectation setting conversations,

112
00:04:44.399 --> 00:04:47.940
first connect with your local HR partner to get guidance on how to approach

113
00:04:47.940 --> 00:04:51.140
the compensation expectation setting conversation with candidates

114
00:04:51.239 --> 00:04:53.179
and who will have the discussions.

115
00:04:53.720 --> 00:04:54.359
Remember,

116
00:04:54.880 --> 00:04:56.850
These should happen early in the process.

117
00:04:57.160 --> 00:04:58.519
In some instances,

118
00:04:58.799 --> 00:05:02.420
you may be working with a recruiter or a member of your local HR team

119
00:05:02.559 --> 00:05:06.480
to conduct initial screens of candidates prior to more formal interviews.

120
00:05:06.679 --> 00:05:07.760
If this is the case,

121
00:05:07.959 --> 00:05:10.269
the recruiter or HR partner may be able to

122
00:05:10.269 --> 00:05:13.630
have initial expectation setting conversations during this process.

123
00:05:13.720 --> 00:05:15.179
In other situations,

124
00:05:15.279 --> 00:05:15.890
you.

125
00:05:16.299 --> 00:05:17.260
As the manager,

126
00:05:17.549 --> 00:05:20.500
may be the first point of contact with prospective candidates,

127
00:05:20.709 --> 00:05:22.809
and you may be the one to have this conversation.

128
00:05:22.950 --> 00:05:24.630
If you are leading this conversation,

129
00:05:25.070 --> 00:05:27.160
take time to connect with your HR partner

130
00:05:27.390 --> 00:05:28.730
to make sure you understand.

131
00:05:29.339 --> 00:05:31.160
The market-based salary range,

132
00:05:31.459 --> 00:05:32.619
the budgeted range,

133
00:05:33.019 --> 00:05:35.519
impacts to the range based on the candidate's work location,

134
00:05:36.089 --> 00:05:37.880
if they would be a remote employee,

135
00:05:38.380 --> 00:05:42.010
and any special considerations that are unique to your school or unit.

136
00:05:42.140 --> 00:05:42.559
Often,

137
00:05:42.859 --> 00:05:44.420
it is helpful to draft a brief script

138
00:05:44.420 --> 00:05:46.929
or talking points to use during these conversations.

139
00:05:47.250 --> 00:05:48.940
This will ensure information is delivered

140
00:05:48.940 --> 00:05:51.399
accurately and consistently to all candidates.

141
00:05:51.540 --> 00:05:55.130
Your HR partner can help you with this as you prepare for these conversations.

142
00:05:55.959 --> 00:05:58.609
If you are leading these expectation setting discussions,

143
00:05:58.970 --> 00:06:02.130
remember to have the conversation early in the interview process.

144
00:06:02.329 --> 00:06:04.609
The next type of compensation conversation

145
00:06:04.609 --> 00:06:06.450
comes when you have selected a candidate

146
00:06:06.570 --> 00:06:08.489
and are ready to make an offer of employment.

147
00:06:08.649 --> 00:06:09.929
In this conversation,

148
00:06:10.130 --> 00:06:11.869
the candidate may wish to negotiate.

149
00:06:12.230 --> 00:06:12.459
So,

150
00:06:12.470 --> 00:06:14.059
preparation will be important.

151
00:06:14.429 --> 00:06:15.049
First,

152
00:06:15.309 --> 00:06:17.369
connect with your local HR partner.

153
00:06:17.589 --> 00:06:19.630
They will help you determine what the initial offer

154
00:06:19.630 --> 00:06:22.529
range should be based on a number of factors,

155
00:06:22.940 --> 00:06:24.230
including those we discussed in the

156
00:06:24.230 --> 00:06:26.890
internal comparability section of this training.

157
00:06:27.079 --> 00:06:29.309
This means you should also consult with your local HR

158
00:06:29.309 --> 00:06:32.170
partner to consider internal equity when determining the offer.

159
00:06:32.390 --> 00:06:35.429
Take the time to understand how the proposed offer compares to.

160
00:06:35.510 --> 00:06:38.170
Existing employees in the same or similar job

161
00:06:38.309 --> 00:06:39.890
at the same level and work location.

162
00:06:40.470 --> 00:06:40.980
Next,

163
00:06:41.230 --> 00:06:43.000
you should work with your HR partner to

164
00:06:43.000 --> 00:06:45.470
understand where you have flexibility to negotiate with

165
00:06:45.470 --> 00:06:47.390
the candidate and the levers that you have

166
00:06:47.390 --> 00:06:49.970
at your disposal as part of that negotiation.

167
00:06:50.149 --> 00:06:52.589
Align with your HR partner on what this could look

168
00:06:52.589 --> 00:06:55.140
like and the specific tools you have at your disposal.

169
00:06:55.230 --> 00:06:58.809
You should also understand where you have the authority to make a final decision.

170
00:06:59.160 --> 00:07:00.920
And where you need additional approval.

171
00:07:01.079 --> 00:07:01.799
For example,

172
00:07:02.119 --> 00:07:05.700
there is likely a compensation range that you will be able to negotiate

173
00:07:05.700 --> 00:07:08.239
within while still maintaining internal equity

174
00:07:08.239 --> 00:07:10.279
based on the specific candidate's knowledge,

175
00:07:10.600 --> 00:07:12.000
skills and abilities.

176
00:07:12.230 --> 00:07:16.100
An offer outside of that specific range may not be appropriate for the candidate.

177
00:07:16.279 --> 00:07:17.119
In this case,

178
00:07:17.369 --> 00:07:19.519
any requests outside of that specific range

179
00:07:19.519 --> 00:07:21.679
will likely need further discussion with HR

180
00:07:21.799 --> 00:07:24.100
or other approvers within your school or unit

181
00:07:24.239 --> 00:07:27.260
so they can help balance pay equity with the needs of the business.

182
00:07:27.665 --> 00:07:28.325
Finally,

183
00:07:28.585 --> 00:07:30.346
it will be important as part of this

184
00:07:30.346 --> 00:07:33.746
process to review Stanford's work agreement process and policies

185
00:07:33.946 --> 00:07:36.425
so you can share these during your discussions with the candidate.

186
00:07:36.645 --> 00:07:37.096
Again,

187
00:07:37.286 --> 00:07:39.805
preparing talking points for yourself can be useful

188
00:07:40.066 --> 00:07:43.085
and your HR partner may be able to assist you with this.

189
00:07:43.265 --> 00:07:46.135
The next type of compensation conversation we will discuss

190
00:07:46.135 --> 00:07:49.936
today is annual merit or base salary increase conversations.

191
00:07:50.055 --> 00:07:52.216
If you are involved in salary planning,

192
00:07:52.466 --> 00:07:56.631
you will receive specific details and guidance from your Local HR department.

193
00:07:56.962 --> 00:07:57.571
Typically,

194
00:07:57.832 --> 00:08:00.231
the process begins in April or May each year

195
00:08:00.231 --> 00:08:03.361
and increases are communicated to employees in late August.

196
00:08:03.721 --> 00:08:06.691
These discussions require preparation so you can properly

197
00:08:06.691 --> 00:08:08.791
explain the decision and the drivers behind it.

198
00:08:08.951 --> 00:08:10.571
As you prepare for these discussions,

199
00:08:10.731 --> 00:08:14.332
connect with your local HR partner to ensure you have the information you need,

200
00:08:14.691 --> 00:08:16.231
including the approved salary letter.

201
00:08:16.481 --> 00:08:18.731
These conversations are informed by a few

202
00:08:18.731 --> 00:08:21.071
factors that consider both the big picture

203
00:08:21.291 --> 00:08:22.372
and the local picture.

204
00:08:22.691 --> 00:08:23.712
For the big picture,

205
00:08:24.130 --> 00:08:27.929
You will want to take time to understand Stanford's compensation philosophy

206
00:08:28.130 --> 00:08:30.179
and the factors taken into consideration for

207
00:08:30.179 --> 00:08:33.219
merit increases for that specific fiscal year.

208
00:08:33.450 --> 00:08:34.349
For the local picture,

209
00:08:34.679 --> 00:08:37.210
it's important to consult with your HR partner to

210
00:08:37.210 --> 00:08:40.409
understand the specific practices and processes for your school.

211
00:08:40.664 --> 00:08:41.183
Unit

212
00:08:41.304 --> 00:08:44.354
or department that influence merit increase decisions.

213
00:08:44.585 --> 00:08:46.844
It's also important to take time to refresh your knowledge

214
00:08:46.844 --> 00:08:50.244
of the employees' annual performance review and come to the conversation

215
00:08:50.385 --> 00:08:53.525
with specific examples of their performance and contributions

216
00:08:53.664 --> 00:08:56.804
that informed the employees merit-based increase for that year.

217
00:08:57.590 --> 00:09:00.760
To ensure you have the information you need on hand during the discussion,

218
00:09:01.000 --> 00:09:03.260
it may be helpful to have written notes or talking

219
00:09:03.260 --> 00:09:05.669
points for each direct report to guide your discussion.

220
00:09:05.919 --> 00:09:07.239
Once you gather the details,

221
00:09:07.309 --> 00:09:09.799
you need to have informed and thoughtful conversations

222
00:09:09.799 --> 00:09:12.549
with your direct reports about merit-based increases.

223
00:09:12.840 --> 00:09:15.919
You should also take time to consider your approach to the discussion.

224
00:09:16.080 --> 00:09:17.719
Here are a few tips to help guide you.

225
00:09:18.349 --> 00:09:19.789
At the start of the discussion,

226
00:09:20.119 --> 00:09:22.179
take a few minutes to create a safe environment

227
00:09:22.479 --> 00:09:24.619
for an open and transparent conversation.

228
00:09:24.840 --> 00:09:26.520
You can do this by assuring the employee

229
00:09:26.520 --> 00:09:28.549
that the conversation is between you and them,

230
00:09:28.799 --> 00:09:32.140
and you're open to hearing any questions or concerns they may wish to talk about.

231
00:09:32.320 --> 00:09:34.820
It's also important to let them know that they may have questions

232
00:09:34.820 --> 00:09:37.099
that you might not be able to answer in the meeting.

233
00:09:37.440 --> 00:09:38.159
After all,

234
00:09:38.559 --> 00:09:40.219
you're not a compensation expert.

235
00:09:40.429 --> 00:09:43.340
Let them know that if there's anything you can't answer,

236
00:09:43.640 --> 00:09:47.140
you will take time after the conversation to get the information they need.

237
00:09:47.479 --> 00:09:48.859
And you will follow up with them.

238
00:09:49.119 --> 00:09:49.549
Next,

239
00:09:49.880 --> 00:09:52.190
take a moment to express that the employee is

240
00:09:52.190 --> 00:09:54.880
valued and appreciated as a member of the team.

241
00:09:55.239 --> 00:09:57.479
Name specific contributions they have made

242
00:09:57.479 --> 00:10:00.739
and express appreciation in non-monetary terms.

243
00:10:01.330 --> 00:10:02.559
Once you've set the tone,

244
00:10:02.950 --> 00:10:05.380
share the increase in a way that is clear and direct.

245
00:10:05.799 --> 00:10:07.130
Ground your words in fact,

246
00:10:07.280 --> 00:10:09.580
and don't apologize or rationalize.

247
00:10:10.000 --> 00:10:11.780
You can use the statement shown on the screen,

248
00:10:12.039 --> 00:10:12.840
for example,

249
00:10:13.119 --> 00:10:15.039
to help you communicate the details.

250
00:10:15.570 --> 00:10:18.020
You can also take a moment to provide context.

251
00:10:18.169 --> 00:10:19.159
In this instance,

252
00:10:19.390 --> 00:10:22.090
context may include information about your school,

253
00:10:22.450 --> 00:10:23.010
unit,

254
00:10:23.330 --> 00:10:25.969
or department's approach to performance evaluation.

255
00:10:26.239 --> 00:10:28.869
And how that influences compensation decisions.

256
00:10:29.080 --> 00:10:31.940
Your HR partner can provide you with any contextual

257
00:10:31.940 --> 00:10:33.979
information that may be important for the discussion.

258
00:10:34.159 --> 00:10:38.400
If they ask for information about the compensation of other employees,

259
00:10:38.599 --> 00:10:40.359
it's important to remind them that you are

260
00:10:40.359 --> 00:10:42.559
not able to discuss those details with them.

261
00:10:42.719 --> 00:10:43.760
You are not able,

262
00:10:44.119 --> 00:10:47.059
nor is it appropriate to discuss comparisons of their pay

263
00:10:47.200 --> 00:10:48.500
to other employees.

264
00:10:49.000 --> 00:10:51.559
You can use the statement shown on the screen as a response.

265
00:10:51.820 --> 00:10:52.429
Finally,

266
00:10:52.609 --> 00:10:54.739
it's important to be ready for a reaction.

267
00:10:55.590 --> 00:10:58.109
If they are expressing unhappiness or concern,

268
00:10:58.559 --> 00:11:00.460
take a moment to listen more than you talk.

269
00:11:00.719 --> 00:11:02.580
Don't rush to judgment or defense.

270
00:11:02.719 --> 00:11:06.320
Ask open-ended questions to understand their concerns and questions.

271
00:11:06.479 --> 00:11:09.429
Hear them without interrupting and recognize their emotions,

272
00:11:09.599 --> 00:11:12.710
but avoid apologizing if the increase is less than they hoped.

273
00:11:12.880 --> 00:11:14.080
You can use a statement like,

274
00:11:14.200 --> 00:11:16.979
I hear you and understand it is a concern for you.

275
00:11:17.739 --> 00:11:20.580
My goal is also to help you understand your current pay and

276
00:11:20.580 --> 00:11:22.929
I will do my best to answer the questions you have.

277
00:11:23.299 --> 00:11:23.859
Remember,

278
00:11:24.020 --> 00:11:25.820
if you are not able to answer their

279
00:11:25.820 --> 00:11:27.799
questions or address their concerns in the meeting,

280
00:11:28.099 --> 00:11:28.760
that's OK.

281
00:11:29.119 --> 00:11:30.760
Let them know that you are happy to get

282
00:11:30.760 --> 00:11:33.630
the information they are requesting following the meeting.

283
00:11:33.840 --> 00:11:34.320
However,

284
00:11:34.549 --> 00:11:36.760
don't promise this unless you intend to connect with

285
00:11:36.760 --> 00:11:39.179
your HR partner and follow up with the employee.

286
00:11:39.469 --> 00:11:41.700
If the employee is expressing disappointment,

287
00:11:41.919 --> 00:11:42.539
anger,

288
00:11:42.719 --> 00:11:44.330
or other negative emotions,

289
00:11:44.599 --> 00:11:47.359
and the conversation has ceased to be productive,

290
00:11:47.559 --> 00:11:49.340
it is OK to suggest a break

291
00:11:49.479 --> 00:11:51.419
and to schedule a follow-up conversation.

292
00:11:51.760 --> 00:11:52.299
Finally,

293
00:11:52.530 --> 00:11:56.359
you may find yourself in an employee initiated compensation conversation.

294
00:11:56.590 --> 00:11:57.200
Often,

295
00:11:57.650 --> 00:12:02.000
These conversations come up during an employee one on one or other form of check-in,

296
00:12:02.330 --> 00:12:06.150
and you may not have advanced notice of the specific details they wish to discuss.

297
00:12:06.289 --> 00:12:08.890
If a conversation like this comes up unexpectedly,

298
00:12:09.049 --> 00:12:11.590
it's important to pause to listen to their concerns

299
00:12:11.729 --> 00:12:15.109
and ask clarifying questions to ensure you understand their perspective

300
00:12:15.250 --> 00:12:17.030
and the information they are looking for.

301
00:12:17.210 --> 00:12:20.070
It often helps to write down their questions and concerns

302
00:12:20.210 --> 00:12:21.770
so you can refer back to them if you

303
00:12:21.770 --> 00:12:24.280
need to gather additional information to address them.

304
00:12:24.489 --> 00:12:26.890
Once you understand Their questions and concerns.

305
00:12:27.359 --> 00:12:29.979
Take a moment to evaluate your current knowledge and decide

306
00:12:29.979 --> 00:12:32.359
if you're able to have the conversation in the moment.

307
00:12:32.539 --> 00:12:34.900
If you need time to refresh your compensation knowledge

308
00:12:34.900 --> 00:12:37.609
and connect with your HR partner for more information,

309
00:12:37.940 --> 00:12:38.640
that's OK.

310
00:12:38.820 --> 00:12:40.619
Let the employee know that you've noted their

311
00:12:40.619 --> 00:12:42.059
questions and concerns and that you need to

312
00:12:42.059 --> 00:12:44.059
gather more information to be able to give

313
00:12:44.059 --> 00:12:45.809
them the information they are looking for.

314
00:12:45.979 --> 00:12:46.280
Then,

315
00:12:46.900 --> 00:12:48.419
following the initial conversation,

316
00:12:48.909 --> 00:12:51.159
Schedule a time to connect with your HR partner

317
00:12:51.159 --> 00:12:53.090
and schedule a follow-up meeting with the employee.

318
00:12:53.280 --> 00:12:54.140
In general,

319
00:12:54.440 --> 00:12:56.299
when you come back to the employee,

320
00:12:56.520 --> 00:12:58.320
you will want to make sure you understand

321
00:12:58.320 --> 00:13:00.909
the core principles discussed in today's training,

322
00:13:01.200 --> 00:13:02.099
which include

323
00:13:02.289 --> 00:13:04.200
external market competitiveness,

324
00:13:04.440 --> 00:13:06.099
internal comparability

325
00:13:06.400 --> 00:13:07.679
and performance recognition.

326
00:13:08.390 --> 00:13:10.070
It is also helpful to refresh your

327
00:13:10.070 --> 00:13:12.849
awareness of Stanford's approach to pay transparency

328
00:13:13.030 --> 00:13:16.750
and the specific job description and job market salary range for the employee.

329
00:13:17.030 --> 00:13:20.250
Consult with your local HR partner to determine the specific

330
00:13:20.250 --> 00:13:22.369
information you may wish to discuss with the employee.

331
00:13:22.619 --> 00:13:26.789
If they ask for information about the compensation of other employees,

332
00:13:26.989 --> 00:13:28.450
it's important to remind them

333
00:13:28.590 --> 00:13:31.450
that you are not able to discuss those details with them.

334
00:13:31.710 --> 00:13:35.469
Here are a few additional tips to support pay discussions with employees.

335
00:13:36.140 --> 00:13:36.640
First,

336
00:13:36.940 --> 00:13:39.559
be curious and focus the conversation forward.

337
00:13:39.780 --> 00:13:42.219
When an employee expresses dissatisfaction,

338
00:13:42.580 --> 00:13:46.219
it's an opportunity to understand the employees' perceptions of their pay.

339
00:13:47.000 --> 00:13:49.330
Listen calmly and ask questions like,

340
00:13:49.799 --> 00:13:52.219
what about this pay range feels inaccurate to you?

341
00:13:52.440 --> 00:13:55.150
What has changed to suggest that your pay should increase?

342
00:13:55.520 --> 00:13:57.940
Are there things other than pay that are valuable to you?

343
00:13:58.280 --> 00:14:00.280
What is most important or valuable to you?

344
00:14:00.770 --> 00:14:02.760
What are your expectations for your pay growth?

345
00:14:02.900 --> 00:14:06.109
It's also important to offer non-monetary recognition

346
00:14:06.489 --> 00:14:08.549
that reflects how you value the employee.

347
00:14:08.719 --> 00:14:11.919
This could include opportunities to work on more challenging projects,

348
00:14:12.260 --> 00:14:13.289
be a team lead,

349
00:14:13.650 --> 00:14:15.289
work on a visible initiative,

350
00:14:15.570 --> 00:14:17.369
or take time to gain new knowledge,

351
00:14:17.690 --> 00:14:18.960
skills or abilities.

352
00:14:19.169 --> 00:14:20.510
You can ask questions like,

353
00:14:21.130 --> 00:14:23.729
what aspect of your current job do you enjoy most?

354
00:14:23.929 --> 00:14:26.469
Are there other positions of interest to you where you feel

355
00:14:26.469 --> 00:14:29.580
you can expand or grow in ways you enjoy most?

356
00:14:29.950 --> 00:14:33.140
Is there a career path or career job you aspire to?

357
00:14:33.309 --> 00:14:36.950
You can also be clear about what is or is not possible in their current role.

358
00:14:37.289 --> 00:14:38.130
For example,

359
00:14:38.390 --> 00:14:40.390
it may not be possible for the employee to

360
00:14:40.390 --> 00:14:42.770
achieve their desired pay level in their current job.

361
00:14:42.909 --> 00:14:43.909
If that's the case,

362
00:14:44.349 --> 00:14:45.469
be honest about that

363
00:14:45.750 --> 00:14:48.429
and suggest a conversation about career growth in

364
00:14:48.429 --> 00:14:50.929
support of the employee's long term earning potential.